  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Implementation\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding a target value within a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Compare the target with the middle element.\n2. If they match, return the index.\n3. If the target is smaller, discard the right half and search the left half.\n4. If the target is larger, discard the left half and search the right half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis halving strategy gives it **O(log n)** time complexity \u2014 dramatically faster than linear search for large datasets.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\nThe standard binary search returns *any* matching index when duplicates exist. But what if we need the **first** occurrence? That's where `lower_bound` comes in.\n\n**Definition:** `lower_bound(arr, target)` returns the **leftmost index** `i` such that `arr[i] >= target`. If no such element exists (all elements are smaller than `target`), it returns `len",
